- Record what you checked:

    drover audit {{ .ID }} <claimed|reviewed|verified> --code-by <who> [--reviewer <name>] "<what you re-checked and what you found>"

  `verified` means every numbered requirement above was traced to shipped
  code, verify commands included. Drop to `reviewed` only when you could not trace one.

  `--reviewer` names who looked and is optional, but omit it and a person who
  read the diff by hand records the same absence as nobody having looked.
  Nothing defaults it.

  Finding one unmet does not lower the level: the row records how hard you
  looked, not how the item scored. What happens to the gap depends on whether
  the work can still be changed. Before the merge the three `BRANCH-` cases
  above go back through a request for changes and `FIX-IN-REVIEW` is corrected in this pass;
  either way nothing is filed. After the merge the branch is gone, so each
  `BRANCH-` case becomes a follow-up filed against its owner, `FIX-IN-REVIEW` has no
  branch left to commit to and becomes `NEW-ITEM`, and the row still records
  that someone traced them all.

  `--code-by` names who wrote the code: `agent` for the implement session drover
  launched, a person's name, or a comma list (`agent,sam`) for mixed
  authorship. `agent` resolves per session, so this review session is not the
  `agent` that wrote the code unless it is also the session that implemented
  it. drover refuses `verified` when you match an entry, and names the entry:
  whoever wrote the code cannot certify every requirement against it. A
  `FIX-IN-REVIEW` correction no numbered requirement traces to is not code the
  level certifies, so it does not make this session an author of it. Anything
  else does.
